==English==
===Etymology===
Originally The Bailey, a street running outside the western wall of medieval London; from the word "bailey," a Middle English term for the outermost wall or court of a fortification.  The courthouse built nearby in the 16th century, alongside old Newgate Prison, took its name from the street.  The modern facility on the site is officially known as the Central Criminal Court.

